import java.util.* ;
import java.io.*; 
public class Solution {

    public static int beautifulIndex(int N, int[] A)
    {
        int total = Arrays.stream(A).sum();
        int prefixSum = 0;

        for(int i = 0; i < N; i++){
            int rightsum = total - prefixSum - A[i];

            if(prefixSum == rightsum){
                return i+1;
            }
            prefixSum += A[i];
        }
        return -1;
    }
}

You are given an array ‘A’ of length ‘N’. You say an index ‘i’ is beautiful if the sum of the first ‘i - 1’ elements of the array ‘A’ equals the sum of the last ‘N - i’ elements of the array ‘A’,
where ‘i’ is in 1-based indexing. Now you wonder which is the leftmost beautiful index.
Note: If you select the first index, then the sum of the prefix will be ‘0’, and if you select the last index, then the sum of the suffix will be ‘0’.
Note: You have to print the index in 1-based indexing.

For example:
Let’s say the array ‘A’ = [1, 3, 1, 5], then if we select index ‘2’, the sum of the prefix is ‘1’, and the sum of the suffix is 1 + 5 = 6. Since the sum is not the same, hence index ‘2’ is not a beautiful index.

T.C => O(N)
S.C => O(1)
